---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";

// Identity is Hugging Face's, and this page says so rather than offering an
// account of our own.
//
// **There is no sign-up here.** The author's sketch was "Log in with hf / no hf
// account? create one and redirect them to hf", and both halves of that are a
// redirect to Hugging Face. This registry creates nothing, stores no password
// and issues no identity, so a page with a "Sign up" heading on it would be
// advertising an account model `V2.md` section 1 specifically declines: `author`
// stays a free namespace string, and claiming is a separate object. The home
// page's Account section came out for that reason. This must not put it back.
//
// **Signing in on its own gives somebody nothing**, which is why the page is
// three steps rather than one button. Holding an account, signing in, and
// claiming a namespace are different acts and only the last one is visible in
// the corpus. A page that leads with the button implies the button is the point.
//
// **No session handling here, and that is still the case after the return leg
// landed.** `astro.config.mjs` is `output: "static"` with no adapter and this
// page ships no script of its own. The session, the exchange and the form all
// live on `/signed-in/`, which is one page, so there is one place in this build
// that holds a session rather than two. This page explains; that page does.
//
// The reason to keep them apart is the reason the Account section came out: a
// page that leads with a button implies the button is the point, and signing in
// on its own still gives somebody nothing. Holding an account, signing in and
// claiming a namespace are three acts and only the last is visible in the
// corpus.
//
// **The copy is drafted, not dictated.** Structure and accuracy are mine; the
// sentences are the author's to rewrite.

const HF_CREATE = "https://huggingface.co/join";
---

<Base title="Sign in" trail={[{ text: "controlbun", href: "/" }]} synthetic="none">
  <h1 slot="heading">Sign in</h1>

  <p class="lede">
    Identity here is Hugging Face's. This registry holds no password and has
    nothing to sign up for.
  </p>

  <p class="banner">
    <strong>Signing in publishes nothing and submits nothing.</strong> It
    produces one thing: a dated reading of what Hugging Face says about your
    account at that moment, which you keep. Sending a submission is a separate
    press on the page after this one, and reading this site is metered by
    nothing and needs none of it.
  </p>

  <h2>Three steps, and the middle one is the smallest</h2>

  <ol class="steps">
    <li>
      <h3>Hold a Hugging Face account</h3>
      <p>
        Most people publishing a steering direction already have one, because
        that is where the weights and the artifacts live. If you do not,
        Hugging Face registers it, not us.
      </p>
      <p><a href={HF_CREATE}>Create a Hugging Face account</a></p>
    </li>

    <li>
      <h3>Sign in with it</h3>
      <p>
        Hugging Face asks you whether to let this registry read who you are. If
        you agree, what comes back is an opaque subject id, the handle you hold
        at that moment, and, if you grant it, the organizations Hugging Face
        says you belong to. No password reaches this site, because none is ever
        sent here.
      </p>
      <p>
        <a href="/signed-in/">Sign in with Hugging Face</a>{" "}
        <span class="note">
          That page sends you to Hugging Face and receives you back. It holds
          the reading in the tab and nowhere else, so closing the tab ends it.
          Signing in on its own writes no submission; that is a button further
          down that page.
        </span>
      </p>
    </li>

    <li>
      <h3>Claim a namespace</h3>
      <p>
        This is the act with meaning. A claim binds the account to a namespace
        and is recorded with its date and its evidence, so a reader can weigh
        it. Everything published under a namespace stands whether or not anyone
        has claimed it, and a namespace nobody has claimed is the ordinary
        state rather than a gap.
      </p>
      <p>
        More than one account can claim the same namespace. Both claims stand,
        both carry their own evidence, and this registry does not decide
        between them.
      </p>
    </li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Your namespace is your handle, and there is no field for it</h2>

  <p>
    When you submit, the namespace is the handle Hugging Face reported when you
    signed in, and the database writes it from your session rather than from
    anything you typed. It is shown to you, not offered:
    no box, and no default to type over. Somebody signed in under one handle
    cannot publish under a name belonging to somebody else, and a dispute about
    who really holds a name is Hugging Face's to settle rather than ours.
  </p>

  <p class="note">
    What that makes impossible, stated because a rule that cannot name its cost
    has not been thought through: publishing under a pseudonym, under a lab name
    you hold no account for, or into one namespace shared by several people who
    each sign in as themselves. The last is a real shape, and today the answer
    is that a group claims a namespace rather than submits into one. An author
    string written down by somebody indexing published work is unaffected, and
    stays a free string.
  </p>

  <h2>A membership is something a provider said on a day</h2>

  <p>
    If Hugging Face reports your organizations, what gets recorded is what it
    said and when it said it. Nothing rechecks it afterwards, so nothing here
    will ever claim you belong to an organization now. People join and leave;
    a membership read once and held forever goes stale quietly while still
    looking like a fact. Checking again means signing in again, which writes a
    new observation beside the old one rather than replacing it.
  </p>

  <h2>Why Hugging Face</h2>

  <p>
    Because that is where the artifacts already are, and because it can answer
    the organization question, which a plain login cannot. It is the provider
    this was built against and not a list of permitted ones. A claim records
    the provider string it was made with and nothing anywhere compares that
    string against a set of approved names, so another provider is a
    configuration rather than a change to the schema.
  </p>

  <p class="note">
    A claim binds the provider's subject id and not the handle. Handles are
    renameable on every provider, so a claim bound to a handle would either
    break when somebody renames or follow the name to whoever registers it
    next. The handle is recorded as of the claim date, and is not what anything
    looks up by.
  </p>

  <h2>What is missing, in one sentence</h2>

  <p>
    A way in for somebody who is not on Hugging Face, and a way to correct a
    submission that is not another submission. What a submission does not wait
    for is a person's opinion of it: it lands, and it appears on the site when
    the author rebuilds and publishes, because the corpus is a file in git and
    that is what the falsifier checks.{" "}
    <a href="/about/">What this registry is and where it came from</a>.
  </p>
</Base>
